<p><a href="https://www.girlonthenet.com">Girl on the Net</a> (I will call her GOTN moving forward) <a href="https://www.girlonthenet.com/blog/cornell/">wrote</a> about <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Cornell_University_rape_allegations">the Cornell rape allegations</a>. There&rsquo;s plenty to read on her website and in the Wikipedia page if you want the details — it&rsquo;s unfortunately a familiar story in American colleges.</p>
<p>What moved me to think was this part:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Far harder to start an awkward conversation. One-on-one or in the group chat with your guy friends. Somewhere there aren&rsquo;t any women to show off to.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yeah. I know exactly what she&rsquo;s talking about. I&rsquo;m an IT guy. I work with IT folks who are still about 90% men, and yes, I&rsquo;ve heard some messed-up things. I&rsquo;m sure I&rsquo;m not the only one.</p>
<p>Sexist jokes happen because sex happens, and some people are less mature than others. Some are just trying to break the ice, or be &ldquo;one of the guys,&rdquo; or god knows what. It doesn&rsquo;t matter. Some of the things I&rsquo;ve heard made me cringe, and I knew they were wrong the moment I heard them. I didn&rsquo;t do anything and just moved on.</p>
<p>The why is easy to answer. The people who said the cringy things are people I work with day in and day out. These are people I rely on. Some are my friends at work. Standing up and saying something won&rsquo;t just be awkward, it would be a showstopper. It would derail a whole meeting that is otherwise important, and it might scare these people off from talking to me openly again, because &ldquo;I can&rsquo;t take a joke&rdquo; or whatever. It wouldn&rsquo;t be an understatement to say that it would damage my job.</p>
<p>The other part of rape culture GOTN talks about in this post is the hero flaw: men wait to be heroes and stop the evil villain who is about to do something &ldquo;rapeful&rdquo; to an innocent poor woman, and they will swoop in to save the day, just like in the movies. Obviously, this doesn&rsquo;t usually happen, so if you&rsquo;re waiting on this, you&rsquo;re probably not going to do anything in the end.</p>
<p>Thing is, if something like this happened around me, I&rsquo;m not sure I&rsquo;d be able to act. I&rsquo;m not a big guy; I&rsquo;m a lifelong geek who was bullied in school growing up (worth mentioning here while we&rsquo;re on the topic that one of the most persistent bullies was a girl). But reality is not a fantasy, and I know similar situations from my past when I had to do something, and I did. And it was probably as remote from being a hero as you could imagine.</p>
<p>It&rsquo;s scary, and you need to fight <em>against</em> your instinct on both counts. Your instinct tells you to shut up, laugh along, or move to another car—There is no hero instinct<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>GOTN and probably others who read this post would say it&rsquo;s nothing and doesn&rsquo;t really help, and they are probably right. I&rsquo;d just like to say it doesn&rsquo;t help <em>enough</em>. It&rsquo;s something. I had her article saved for two weeks and it was something I wasn&rsquo;t sure I wanted to write about. I could have just shrugged and move on, but I didn&rsquo;t at the end. And even though I don&rsquo;t think folks would believe me if I say it&rsquo;s not about guilt, I&rsquo;ll say it anyway. It&rsquo;s not about guilt. It&rsquo;s just something worth saying that needs to be said.</p>
<p>Maybe I can&rsquo;t be as obnoxious as GOTN would like me to be. After all, my connections at work are very important to me (as a manager, that&rsquo;s often what gets stuff done). But her post made me think, and I might be able to do something more next time. Maybe, if you read all the way to this point, you would also. And while it&rsquo;s not enough — it&rsquo;s something.</p>
